# Character count

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

Character count tells people how long a text answer may be and how much they have left. Use it for a bounded dashboard note or message field where the system that receives the text has a documented limit.

It is a Civica pattern built on Home Assistant’s text boxes. It is not a Home Assistant element. This page covers:

- Character count
  - Civica Character Count Pattern

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

1. A label that names the answer.
2. A hint above the box that states the limit before anyone types.
3. The text box.
4. A message below the box that shows how many characters are left.
5. When the text is too long, an error message in plain words, with the text kept in the box.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-civica-character-count-pattern">Civica Character Count Pattern</h3>

Home Assistant’s `ha-textarea` and `ha-input` accept a `maxlength` attribute. That is a hard stop. Typing and pasting are cut off at the limit. Neither shows a count of the characters left.

The Civica pattern differs in two ways. It shows a message under the box, and it lets people keep editing past the limit so they can shorten their own words. A live count like this is example markup or custom card code. Home Assistant does not provide it.

What the theme does set, on the text box itself:

- Border, background, text and placeholder colours, through the `wa-form-control-*` keys. The border is 2px wide with square corners.
- The focus ring: 3px wide, offset 2px. It is black in the light theme and yellow in the dark theme.
- Hint text uses `secondary-text-color`, and the error message uses `error-color`.

The theme has no setting for a counter. If you need one, use the hint and the error message below to carry the limit in words.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- Stating the limit first lets people plan their answer, instead of finding out as they type.
- Keeping over-limit text editable means no one loses their own words.
- Home Assistant’s `maxlength` is reliable but gives no feedback. People may not notice that their text was cut off.
- The count is not native, so the hint and error message must carry the same information in words.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use it only when a real limit applies. Do not invent a limit to make a card fit. If a storage restriction is unnecessary, raise it instead of asking people to shorten useful instructions.

- Write the limit in the hint before the field, such as “You can enter up to 120 characters”.
- Do not block pasting. Let people paste the full answer and then shorten it.
- Say how far over they are, in plain words: “You have 12 characters too many”.
- Keep the text in the box after an error. Do not make people type it again.
- Avoid a countdown that feels like pressure. A calm message under the box is enough.
- Count the same units in the field and on the server, including emoji and pasted text.

A household note is not a message to a support team. Say who will read it and why.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: link the hint and message to the field.
- 3.3.1 Error Identification, 3.3.3 Error Suggestion and 3.3.7 Redundant Entry.
- 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions: state the limit up front.
- 4.1.3 Status Messages: announce the count as a status, not on every keystroke.
- 1.4.10 Reflow: check the field at 320 CSS pixels.

Test pasting, deleting, emoji and an over-limit save. Use the [Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html) checks, in light and dark modes. For the field itself, see [Textarea](textarea.html), and for the wording of the limit, [Hint text](hint-text.html) and [Error message](error-message.html).

